  {
   "repository": "vercel-labs/agent-browser",
   "url": "https://github.com/vercel-labs/agent-browser",
   "trending_rank": 10,
   "description": "A ==native Rust CLI that gives an AI agent a browser== it drives one shell command at a time. snapshot returns the page as a compact accessibility tree with refs such as @e1; the agent clicks or fills a ref, reads the page again, and repeats. A background daemon keeps the browser open over the Chrome DevTools Protocol, and the tool also offers screenshots, network and storage inspection, tracing, an MCP server and versioned agent skills.",
   "why_interesting": "First time on our list (its sibling agent-desktop was featured on 3 and 5 November). Short text snapshots are a cheaper, more checkable alternative to screenshot-driven browsing, and ==a click fails instead of reporting success when the target is covered or hidden==. v0.41.2 (3 November) lets agents load one skill reference at a time and adds a warning when a newer stable Chrome lists CVE fixes.",
   "caveat": "Its first run ==downloads Chrome for Testing==, and Linux setup can install system libraries. An agent with a live browser can read and act on anything that browser can reach, including logged-in sessions if you attach to your own Chrome via CDP, so ==keep it on a separate profile== and use the documented domain allowlists and action policies. Pre-1.0 releases arrive every few days, so pin a version. We did not install it.",
   "category": "AI agents / browser automation",
   "language": "Rust",
   "license": "Apache-2.0",
